Fixtures for the severity gates. Plain text rather than Markdown on purpose: everything under
testdata/ is excluded from the repo's own markdownlint and link checks, and a .md here would be
the one file in the tree neither of them can see.

Real output, captured from the pinned scanners
----------------------------------------------
Every file below except trivy-secret.json is what the tool actually printed - the gates are
parsers, and a parser tested only against hand-written input tests the author's idea of the
format rather than the format.

  trivy-alpine.json            trivy 0.74.0, `image --scanners vuln --list-all-pkgs --format
                               json` against docker.io/library/alpine:3.19. 15 packages,
                               10 vulnerabilities, of which exactly one is fixable HIGH
                               (CVE-2026-40200, musl + musl-utils). The rest are MEDIUM/LOW,
                               which is the below-threshold case.
  trivy-alpine-accepted.json   the same scan re-run with --ignorefile naming a .trivyignore.yaml
                               that accepts CVE-2026-40200. The finding is GONE from the JSON:
                               that is the whole acceptance mechanism, captured rather than
                               assumed. When the expiry passes it comes back and the gate fails
                               again, with no code change.
  trivy-no-packages.json       trivy 0.74.0 against docker.io/library/hello-world: no OS, no
                               packages, no findings, exit 0. This is the shape Project
                               Hummingbird's Red Hat image produces for a different reason
                               (ID="hummingbird" is in no distro map), and the reason the gate
                               raises a workflow warning instead of reporting a clean image.
                               See docs/container.md.
  osv-alpine.json              osv-scanner 2.6.0, `scan image --archive` over the same image
                               exported with `podman save --format docker-archive`. The same
                               single blocking finding, as ALPINE-CVE-2026-40200 at CVSS 8.1,
                               and the same below-threshold remainder at 2.5-4.8. It also
                               repeats every finding across several results, which is what the
                               gate's deduplication is for.
  osv-alpine-accepted.json     the same scan with --config naming an osv-scanner.toml whose
                               ignoreUntil covers ALPINE-CVE-2026-40200. Gone from the JSON,
                               exactly as with Trivy.

Derived, and why
----------------
  trivy-alpine-unfixed.json    trivy-alpine.json with CVE-2026-40200's FixedVersion cleared and
  osv-alpine-unfixed.json      osv-alpine.json with ALPINE-CVE-2026-40200's `fixed` range events
                               removed.

Neither image to hand carried an unfixed HIGH, and "fixable" is the field each gate keys on, so
the honest way to exercise it is to take the fix away from a real finding rather than to invent
a report. Everything else in those two files is untouched real output.

  trivy-secret.json            hand-written, and the one file here that is not real output.

A genuine capture would mean committing a real-looking credential to this repository, which the
gitleaks job scans on every push - the fixture would either fail CI or have to be excluded from
it, and an exclusion in a secret scanner is a worse thing to own than a hand-written fixture. It
carries only the fields the gate reads (RuleID, Title, Severity) and no match text at all. It
exists because a HIGH/CRITICAL secret has no fixed version to have, so it must block anyway -
`--ignore-unfixed` applies to vulnerabilities only, and a credential baked into an image is the
finding you least want silently filtered.

Refreshing these
----------------
Don't, unless the format changes. These are frozen inputs, not a live scan: a refresh that
happens to drop the one blocking finding turns several tests green for the wrong reason. If the
scanners' JSON shape does change, recapture with the commands named above and re-read the
counts asserted in scan_test.go.
